---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'samsung-moisture-detected-warning')!;
const url = `https://fixthespeaker.com/guides/${guide.slug}`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: guide.title,
  description: guide.description,
  datePublished: guide.published,
  dateModified: guide.updated,
  mainEntityOfPage: url,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Dev', url: 'https://fixthespeaker.com/about' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://fixthespeaker.com/#organization' },
};
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${guide.title} | Fix The Speaker`}
  description={guide.description}
  heading={guide.title}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  updated={guide.updated}
  localized={false}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> unplug the charger, shake the water out of the port, and give it time. Samsung
    says water in the charging port normally evaporates in about one to two hours. If you need power before then,
    use a wireless charger once the phone is dry on the outside.
  </p>

  <h2>What the warning means</h2>
  <p>
    Water-resistant Galaxy phones check the USB-C port for moisture when you plug in a cable. If they find any,
    they block wired charging and show a water drop icon or a "Moisture detected" message, and some models also
    sound an alarm until you unplug. Samsung explains this as a way to prevent corrosion: charging through a wet
    port can damage the contacts.
  </p>
  <p>
    In other words, the warning is the phone protecting itself. It is not a fault by itself, and you should not
    try to get around it while the port is still wet.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Unplug the cable straight away.</strong> Do not keep plugging it back in to "test" the port. Each try
      pushes current through a wet connector.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shake the water out.</strong> Hold the phone with the USB-C port facing down and shake it gently a few
      times. Wipe the outside with a dry, soft cloth.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rinse first if it was not plain water.</strong> For sea water, pool water, or a drink, Samsung's advice
      is to rinse with fresh water and then dry the phone thoroughly with a dry, soft cloth. Salt and sugar left to
      dry inside the port are what cause corrosion later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leave it somewhere dry and airy.</strong> Samsung says the port normally dries in about one to two
      hours. A room with moving air helps more than a closed drawer or a bowl of rice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Need to charge now? Use wireless charging.</strong> Samsung notes that wireless charging is not affected
      by moisture in the charging port, on phones that support it. Dry the back of the phone first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Try the cable again later.</strong> If the warning is gone, charge normally.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What not to do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not push anything into the port.</strong> Samsung's page is clear about this: no cotton buds, pins,
      or tissue corners. They can bend the contacts or pack lint deeper.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use heat.</strong> A hair dryer, oven, or radiator can damage the battery and soften the
      adhesive that keeps water out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not blast it with compressed air.</strong> Samsung advises against air guns around the speaker and
      microphone holes, because strong air pressure can damage the parts inside.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not charge with wet hands</strong> or with the phone sitting somewhere it could get wet again.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If the phone also sounds muffled</h2>
  <p>
    The bottom speaker sits right next to the USB-C port on most Galaxy phones, so it often gets wet at the same
    time. Water held in the speaker grille makes calls and music sound quiet or crackly. While the port is drying,
    you can hold the phone with the speaker facing down and play a low tone at full volume, which can help shake
    droplets out of the grille. Our <a href="/eject-water-samsung-galaxy">Samsung water eject page</a> does this in
    the browser. It only helps the speaker grille; it does not dry the charging port, so keep waiting before you
    plug in.
  </p>

  <h2>When the warning will not go away</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes the message keeps coming back even though the phone has been dry for hours, or it appears when the
    phone has not been near water at all. Some common reasons:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Lint or dust in the port.</strong> Pocket lint packed into the USB-C port can hold moisture or confuse
      the sensor. Look into the port with a torch. If you see debris, a phone repair shop or service centre can clean
      it safely.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A damaged or dirty cable.</strong> Try a different cable and charger. If the warning only appears with
      one cable, the cable is the problem.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Corrosion.</strong> If salt water or a drink dried inside the port, the contacts may be corroded. This
      needs a repair, not more drying.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If the steps above do not help, Samsung's advice is to contact Samsung Support or visit a Samsung Service
    Centre. Bring the phone, the cable you normally use, and a note of when the warning started.
  </p>

  <h2>Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>Can I just restart the phone to clear the message?</h3>
  <p>
    Restarting may hide the message for a while, but if there really is water in the port it will come back as soon
    as you plug in. Give the port time to dry first.
  </p>
  <h3>Is my phone still water resistant?</h3>
  <p>
    Water resistance is rated for fresh water under test conditions and wears down with age and drops. Treat every
    soaking as a risk, and do not take an older phone swimming.
  </p>
  <h3>Does this apply to Samsung tablets?</h3>
  <p>
    Samsung notes that most of its tablets are not water resistant. If a tablet got wet, turn it off and have it
    checked rather than waiting for a warning.
  </p>

